</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      h3. Context<br/>
In countries where a retailer operates several banners, one person can hold a separate shopper identity in each. Reach, response and control assignment are all computed per identity today.<br/>
<br/>
h3. Problem / opportunity<br/>
Counting identities rather than people overstates reach, and lets the same person be treated as exposed in one banner and held back in another. Both distort the result, and neither is visible without de-duplication.<br/>
<br/>
h3. Objective<br/>
Resolve several banner identities to one person, so reach is counted on people and control assignment cannot conflict.<br/>
<br/>
h3. Business outcome / value<br/>
* Reach figures reflect people rather than cards, which is what an advertiser assumes they are buying<br/>
* Test and control assignment stays sound in multi-banner countries<br/>
* Banner comparisons compare populations of the same shape<br/>
<br/>
h3. In scope<br/>
* Resolution of several banner identities to one person<br/>
* A published de-duplication rate, so the size of the effect is known<br/>
* The person identifier carried through to reporting and to control assignment<br/>
* Applied only in countries where several banners operate<br/>
<br/>
h3. Out of scope<br/>
* Countries operating a single banner, where this adds nothing<br/>
* Any change to how identities are created in the source systems<br/>
<br/>
h3. Acceptance criteria<br/>
* Reach computed on people differs measurably from reach computed on identities, and the difference is reported<br/>
* One person cannot appear in opposing groups for the same campaign<br/>
* The de-duplication rate is published per country<br/>
<br/>
h3. Measure of success<br/>
* De-duplication rate published and stable<br/>
* Zero cases of a person in opposing groups across banners<br/>
* Reach figures for multi-banner countries stated on a person basis<br/>
<br/>
h3. Dependencies and open issues<br/>
# Whether the partner&#39;s platform already de-duplicates across banners, and whether that resolution returns to us<br/>
# Banner populated consistently on the shopper record
  </td>
