</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment"></td>
                                            <td class="description">      h3. Context<br/>
Retail media measurement begins with data arriving from the media partner. Today nothing is ingested: exposure, spend, their attributed results and campaign metadata all sit in the partner&#39;s environment and are read manually if at all.<br/>
<br/>
h3. Problem / opportunity<br/>
Without an automated, reconciled ingestion of what the partner returns, every measurement is a one-off assembled by hand, cannot be reproduced, and cannot be trusted when a number is challenged.<br/>
<br/>
h3. Objective<br/>
Land everything the media partner returns, daily and unattended, in a form that can be reconciled to what we sent and reproduced months later.<br/>
<br/>
h3. Business outcome / value<br/>
* Measurement becomes repeatable rather than a manual exercise per campaign<br/>
* The number of campaigns that can be measured stops depending on analyst availability<br/>
* A past campaign can be re-measured to the same numbers, which is what makes a result defensible<br/>
<br/>
h3. In scope<br/>
* Automated daily ingestion of shopper-level exposure, impressions, clicks and placement<br/>
* Ingestion of media spend, including fixed-placement spend held separately, unioned so cost is complete<br/>
* Ingestion of the partner&#39;s own attributed results, stored separately from our figures and retaining their model name and window<br/>
* Ingestion of campaign metadata, including our programme identifier where the partner writes it<br/>
* A daily snapshot of partner reference data, so historical reporting does not rewrite itself when their dimensions change<br/>
* A load register recording every feed, its row counts and its outcome<br/>
* Re-running a load produces no duplicates<br/>
<br/>
h3. Out of scope<br/>
* Any transformation or interpretation of partner figures. They are stored as received<br/>
* The partner-side work of producing the feed<br/>
* Identity resolution, which is a separate epic<br/>
<br/>
h3. Acceptance criteria<br/>
* A day of exposure, spend and attributed results lands without manual intervention and the load register records it<br/>
* Fixed-placement spend is provably included in the total cost for a campaign<br/>
* A change in partner reference data is visible as two dated snapshots rather than as a silent overwrite<br/>
* Re-running any load leaves the data unchanged<br/>
* Partner attributed figures are retrievable with the model and window they were produced under<br/>
<br/>
h3. Measure of success<br/>
* Daily loads complete unattended for four consecutive weeks<br/>
* Row counts received reconcile to row counts the partner reports sending<br/>
* Zero manual steps between the partner publishing and the data being queryable<br/>
<br/>
h3. Dependencies and open issues<br/>
# Access at a tier that exposes shopper-level data rather than aggregated reporting only<br/>
# A specification of what the return contains, which does not exist today<br/>
# Confirmation of whether we receive transaction-level detail or an aggregated reporting layer
  </td>
